<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chenab</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chenab is a river rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> and flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, one of the five rivers that give the Punjab its name. It joins the other Punjab rivers before reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. Its valley formed part of the agriculturally rich heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p></div>
